<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis VI of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1081">1081</a>–<a href="/years/1137">1137</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Louis the Fat, Louis VI the Fat · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis VI, called the Fat, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1108 to 1137. He strengthened royal authority in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a> by subduing rebellious castellans, aided by his adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suger">Abbot Suger</a> of Saint-Denis. After the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-good">Charles the Good</a> in 1127 he imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-clito">William Clito</a> as count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, though the Flemish towns soon turned against him. He married his son Louis VII to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-vi-of-france.jpg" alt="Louis VI of France" width="320" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Natalis de Wailly (1805-1886) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_VI_of_France.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis was born around 1081 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the son of Philip I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Bertha of Holland. He was knighted in 1098 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-i-count-of-ponthieu">Guy I of Ponthieu</a>, and by 1103 his father had associated him with the government of the kingdom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suger">Abbot Suger</a> of Saint Denis, his adviser and later his biographer, wrote that he tired early of hunting and boyish games and turned to arms. Louis married Lucienne de Rochefort in 1104; the marriage was annulled in 1107. In 1115 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-countess-of-vermandois">Adelaide</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurienne">Maurienne</a>, niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-callixtus-ii">Pope Callixtus II</a>, who was politically active and appears on 45 royal charters of his reign. They had eight children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Louis succeeded at the age of 26. His half-brother prevented him from reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Rheims</a>, so Daimbert, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Sens</a>, crowned him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> on 3 August 1108. The kingdom was then a collection of feudal principalities, and beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a> the king had little authority over the great dukes and counts. Louis spent much of his reign campaigning against the lords whose castles, such as Le Puiset, Châteaufort and Montlhéry, lay around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Clerical writers accused them of charging tolls, waylaying merchants and pilgrims and looting churches. Hugh of Le Puiset rebuilt his castle twice before Louis finally defeated him.</p><p>Louis also fought Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> for twenty years over the strategic castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gisors">Gisors</a>. His forces were routed at the Battle of Bremule in August 1119, and peace followed in 1120 with Gisors left to Henry. In 1124 the Emperor Henry V, Henry I's son-in-law, planned an invasion towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Rheims</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> rallied to Louis, who carried the banner of Saint Denis, and the plan was dropped. After the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-good">Charles the Good</a> in 1127 Louis intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-clito">William Clito</a> elected count, but the towns rebelled against Clito, and Louis had to confirm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thierry-of-alsace">Thierry of Alsace</a> instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 9 April 1137 the dying William X, Duke of Aquitaine, made Louis guardian of his daughter and heiress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a>. Louis married her to his heir, the future Louis VII, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> on 25 July 1137, adding one of the most powerful duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to the Capetian domains. He died of dysentery on 1 August 1137 and was buried in the Basilica of St Denis.</p><p>His weight had grown so great by his forties that campaigning became hard, which gave him the name the Fat. He was the first Capetian to issue ordinances applying to the whole kingdom, and the recognition of his authority led many in French society to see the king as their protector. The marriage in 1128 of the Empress Matilda to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-plantagenet-count-of-anjou">Geoffrey Plantagenet, Count of Anjou</a>, created an alliance that later troubled his successor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis VI of France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later dukes fought for the duchy itself: Henry I of England defeated his brother Robert Curthose at the Battle of Tinchebray in 1106 and <b>Louis VI of France</b> at the Battle of Brémule in 1119.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Louis VI the Fat</b> (r. 1108–1137), advised by Abbot Suger of Saint-Denis, subdued the defiant castellans of the Île-de-France and made the domain safe to travel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Norman Vexin, Henry I of England, duke of Normandy, defeated <b>Louis VI of France</b>, who backed the claim of William Clito.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-20">20 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>William Clito, imposed as Count of Flanders by <b>Louis VI of France</b> after the murder of Charles the Good, defeated his rival Thierry of Alsace at Axspoele.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-21">21 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Château de la Douye, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1117" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis VI of France"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1117">What was here in 1117? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
